    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "ADR-0106 introduced this as an axiom precisely because it is the integer-specific decision the equality-partition reconstruction route needs, and unrestricted excluded middle would have been far too strong. It is now derived from two things a constructor presentation gives and an opaque carrier cannot: injectivity (project the constructor's Nat field with `Int.rec` and rewrite the integer equation into a Nat one) and discrimination (a `Prop`-valued `Int.rec` returning `True` on one constructor and `False` on the other turns `ofNat m = negSucc n` into `False` by transporting `True.intro` across it). The Nat half is decided by `Nat.beq`, whose soundness (`Nat.eq_of_beq_eq_true`) and completeness (`Nat.beq_eq_true_of_eq`) the Nat prelude proves. formal.statement is `Kernel::render_lean` of the admitted type, verbatim from `int_theorem_inventory`.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
      "provenance": {
        "command": "python3 render/producers-py/facts_to_docir.py",
